==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|biker|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|non}} {{term|bikarr||cup|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|osx}} {{term|bikeri||cup|lang=osx}}, from {{etyl|gmw}} {{termx|bikari||beaker|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|LL.}} {{term|bicarium|bīcārium|wine vat, jug|lang=la}}, of disputed origin. Possibly from [[wikipedia|Biblical Hebrew]] בכוס, (the {{wikipedia|Hebrew prefix}} "ב" means "in" and "כוס" means "cup") from the {{etyl|he}} {{term|sc=Hebr|כוס|tr=be'cos|lang=he||in cup, in goblet, in chalice, in glass, [it is] in [the] cup, etc; [contents] in cup, etc|lang=he}}, {{etyl|grc}} {{term|βίκος||tr=bíkos|drinking bowl, earthenware jug, wine jar|lang=grc}}, or from {{etyl|la}} {{term|bacarium||wine vat, vase|lang=la}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|beker||beaker, cup|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Becher||beaker, cup, goblet|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|bæger||beaker|lang=da}}, Italian {{term|bicchiere||cup, glass (for drink)|lang=it}}. See also {{l|en|pitcher}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkə(ɹ)/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːkɚ/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"bi:k@`/}}
* {{rhymes|iːkə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A flat-bottomed [[vessel]], with a [[lip]], used as a [[laboratory]] [[container]].
# A [[drinking]] vessel without a [[handle]], sometimes for the use of [[children]].
# A [[mug]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|drinking vessel without a handle}} [[glass]] (2nd definition)

